From: Andrew Hunt <achunt@bellsouth.net>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 08:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PmLogLib: release the lock when shared memory is unavailable

The library constructor takes the PmLog lock (a lockf() on
/dev/shm/pmloglib.lock) and then creates its SysV shared memory. If shmget() or
shmat() fails it returns with the lock still held - and the lock is only
released when the process exits. Every other process that loads PmLogLib then
blocks in its own constructor waiting for it, forever.

That failure is not hypothetical. Android kernels are built with
CONFIG_SYSVIPC unset, so on a Halium device shmget() fails for every process:
the first webOS service to load the library wedges logging for the whole system,
and every service that logs - the compositor's launcher among them, via
"PmLogCtl def" - hangs at startup. Seen on a Pixel 6a (bluejay): 31 processes
queued on the lock behind an idle LunaBackupAgent, no UI.

Release the lock on both error paths. The library already has a defined state
for "no usable shared memory" - the unrecognized-signature path sets
gGlobalsP = NULL and carries on - and the public context API checks for it, so
logging degrades to its defaults instead of hanging.

PmLogPrvReadConfigs() writes into gGlobalsP through its parser without checking,
and "!loglib loadconf" can reach it at runtime via HandleLogLibCommand, so guard
it too - otherwise this would trade the hang for a crash.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/PmLogLib.c	2026-09-28 07:44:31.575074303 -0400
+++ b/src/PmLogLib.c	2026-09-28 07:44:31.670137554 -0400
@@ -1072,6 +1072,14 @@
     gchar *full_path = NULL;
     bool found_default_conf = false;
 
+    /* No shared globals (shmget/shmat failed at init): the parsers below
+     * write into gGlobalsP, and "!loglib loadconf" can reach them at runtime
+     * through HandleLogLibCommand. */
+    if (gGlobalsP == NULL)
+    {
+        return false;
+    }
+
     dir = g_dir_open(CONFIG_DIR, 0, &error);
     if (!dir) {
         ErrPrint(COMPONENT_PREFIX, "[]", "DIR_OPEN_ERR {\"Error\":\"%s\"}", error->message);
@@ -1222,6 +1230,7 @@
     if (shmid == -1)
     {
         DbgPrint("shmget error: %s\n", strerror(errno));
+        PmLogPrvUnlock();
         return;
     }
 
@@ -1229,6 +1238,7 @@
     if (data == (char*) -1)
     {
         DbgPrint("shmat error: %s\n", strerror(errno));
+        PmLogPrvUnlock();
         return;
     }
 
